When investigating whether a KvBackend-style storage-abstraction trait needs new methods for a new backend, check the trait's arity FIRST (codesearch the trait definition) before assuming the task's premise about missing methods (delete/query) is correct -- often the existing get/put/list_prefix-shaped trait already covers it, and only a NEW implementer struct is needed on the injecting side, zero changes on the trait-owning side. Cross-repo Cargo rlib+cdylib dual crate-type (crate-type=["cdylib","rlib"]) lets one crate ship both a standalone wasm module (extern C entry, host-import ABI) AND a plain Rust dependency embeddable via direct struct injection (trait implemented by the consuming crate) -- these are two live parallel integration paths, not legacy-vs-current, and a migration task scoped to one path must not assume it touches the other.